  "profile": {
    "video_id": "iron-snail:KMQF8w-aRpY",
    "purpose": "Explains why the U.S. Navy replaced the peacoat with the N1 deck jacket during World War II, traces the design steps from earlier zip and hook deck jackets, explains the fabrics and alpaca pile lining, and runs a home-made warmth test on several modern N1 reproductions and other jackets.",
    "video_type": "explainer",
    "thesis": "The N1 deck jacket succeeded the peacoat because its design solved the practical problems sailors faced in freezing salt spray. The video's home-made thermal test suggests the overall jacket design matters as much as the insulating materials.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "The peacoat was dropped because it was heavy, stiff, and hard to put on, take off and move in.",
      "The earlier deck zip jacket was based on an Army winter jacket. Its brass zippers froze shut in salt spray and corroded.",
      "The deck hook jacket used overlapping fabric and side hooks, which the creator says were borrowed from firefighters' coats. It still had exposed metal and storm cuffs that snagged.",
      "The N1 combined a zipper with a button-secured placket, covered the knit storm cuffs, and used a tough ribbed fabric from the grosgrain family, such as jungle cloth or Bedford cord.",
      "Period-accurate N1s use a sliver-knit pile of 50% alpaca and 50% wool. Alpaca insulates well because its fibre is fine and hollow.",
      "Loft can come from brushed wovens, terry fleece, mouton or sliver-knit pile.",
      "The test measures how much a jacket's outside surface warms over a heating pad; a smaller change means better insulation. Results: a Fjällräven jacket barely changed, Heat Straps 10°, Dehen 1920 11°, a '90s Levi's sherpa 13°, The Real McCoy's 15°. The creator stresses a large margin of error.",
      "Iron Heart adds a tightly woven interliner against wind, the same idea used in the Iron Snail Mammoth Jacket.",
      "The N1 was part of a full system with suspender trousers and loops on the jacket. Nylon later replaced it."
    ],
    "outline": [
      {
        "title": "The N1 deck jacket",
        "summary": "Introduces the question of why the Navy swapped the peacoat for the N1 deck jacket and the jackets to be compared: The Real McCoy's, Dehen 1920 and Heat Straps.",
        "first_segment": 0,
        "last_segment": 1
      },
      {
        "title": "Sponsor message",
        "summary": "Skit-style sponsor read for the Reason Labs online security and identity theft protection browser extension.",
        "first_segment": 1,
        "last_segment": 2
      },
      {
        "title": "Testing deck jackets",
        "summary": "Introduces The Real McCoy's N1, on loan from Standard & Strange, and its alpaca/wool pile versus the Heat Straps mouton. Explains the heating-pad and infrared-thermometer test method and the Real McCoy's result of a 15° change.",
        "first_segment": 2,
        "last_segment": 4
      },
      {
        "title": "History of navy gear",
        "summary": "Explains why the peacoat was retired, and the problems with the deck zip jacket (frozen, corroded brass zippers) and the deck hook jacket (exposed metal, snagging storm cuffs).",
        "first_segment": 4,
        "last_segment": 7
      },
      {
        "title": "Jacket design evolution",
        "summary": "Covers the N1's grosgrain-family fabrics (Bedford cord, jungle cloth), the covered storm cuffs, and the zipper-plus-button-placket closure.",
        "first_segment": 8,
        "last_segment": 10
      },
      {
        "title": "Alpaca and pile liners",
        "summary": "Explains loft and the ways to create it (brushing, terry fleece, mouton, sliver knitting), and why alpaca's fine, hollow fibre insulates well.",
        "first_segment": 10,
        "last_segment": 12
      },
      {
        "title": "Modern interpretations",
        "summary": "Looks at the Heat Straps N1 (24 oz canvas, quilted wool batting, lined pockets). Rapid-fire facts cover Iron Heart's interliner, the Mammoth Jacket, the N1 suspender system, and the move to nylon.",
        "first_segment": 12,
        "last_segment": 14
      },
      {
        "title": "Final test results",
        "summary": "Ranks the jackets by warmth: Fjällräven, Heat Straps, Dehen 1920, a '90s Levi's sherpa, then The Real McCoy's. Notes that overall design matters, not just materials.",
        "first_segment": 14,
        "last_segment": 15
      }
    ],
